{"name":"Fine Gold","hex":"#daa826","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fine","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Senses referring to subjective quality.\nOf superior quality.","Of superior quality."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Well, nicely, in a positive, agreeable way.","Finely; elegantly; delicately."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fine"},{"term":"gold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy yellow elemental metal of great value, with atomic number 79.","A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["☉ (alchemy)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fine Gold (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Fine Gold is a 1989 Spanish drama film directed by José Antonio de la Loma and starring Ted Wass, Stewart Granger and Lloyd Bochner. It depicts the ongoing feud between two winemaking families. In the United States, it aired in first run syndication."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fine_Gold_(film)"},{"title":"Fine Gold Creek","blocks":[{"text":"Fine Gold Creek, in Fine Gold Gulch, is a creek in a gulch in Madera County, California that is a river tributary of the San Joaquin River.\nIt is approximately 18 miles (29 km) from its mouth on the San Joaquin through where it has two forks to its headwaters."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fine_Gold_Creek"},{"title":"Fineness","blocks":[{"text":"The fineness of a precious metal object represents the weight of fine metal therein, in proportion to the total weight which includes alloying base metals and any impurities. Alloy metals are added to increase the hardness and durability of coins and jewelry, alter colors, decrease the cost per weight, or avoid the cost of high-purity refinement. For example, copper is added to the precious metal silver to make a more durable alloy for use in coins, housewares and jewelry. Coin silver, which was used for making silver coins in the past, contains 90% silver and 10% copper, by mass. Sterling silver contains 92.5% silver and 7.5% of other metals, usually copper, by mass."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fineness"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/2005_AEPlat_Proof_Rev.png/500px-2005_AEPlat_Proof_Rev.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/2005_AEPlat_Proof_Rev.png/1280px-2005_AEPlat_Proof_Rev.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/2005_AEPlat_Proof_Rev.png/500px-2005_AEPlat_Proof_Rev.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"2005 American Platinum Eagle noting .9995 platinum","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2005_AEPlat_Proof_Rev.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Four_nines_fine_gold.jpeg/500px-Four_nines_fine_gold.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Four_nines_fine_gold.jpeg/500px-Four_nines_fine_gold.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"1 troy ounce of four nines fine gold (999.9)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFour_nines_fine_gold.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Punc_Litva.png/250px-Punc_Litva.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/77/Punc_Litva.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Punc_Litva.png/250px-Punc_Litva.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hallmarks can denote the millesimal fineness. These Lithuanian hallmarks are used for 18 karat gold and 14 karat gold.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APunc_Litva.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/2019_American_Silver_Eagle_and_1943_Walking_Liberty_Half_dollar_side_by_side.jpg/500px-2019_American_Silver_Eagle_and_1943_Walking_Liberty_Half_dollar_side_by_side.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/2019_American_Silver_Eagle_and_1943_Walking_Liberty_Half_dollar_side_by_side.jpg/1280px-2019_American_Silver_Eagle_and_1943_Walking_Liberty_Half_dollar_side_by_side.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/2019_American_Silver_Eagle_and_1943_Walking_Liberty_Half_dollar_side_by_side.jpg/500px-2019_American_Silver_Eagle_and_1943_Walking_Liberty_Half_dollar_side_by_side.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A 2019 American Silver Eagle bullion coin with a fineness of 999 (three nines fine), together with a Walking Liberty half dollar with a fineness of 900 (one nine fine); this latter alloy is also often referred to as 90% silver or coin 